  }
}
```

---

## PRISMA ERROR CODES

```typescript
// Handle common Prisma errors explicitly:
try {
  await db.quiz.create({ data: { shop, slug, title } });
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof Error) {
    if (error.message.includes("P2002")) {
      // Unique constraint violation (e.g., duplicate slug)
      return json({ error: "A quiz with this slug already exists" }, { status: 409 });
    }
    if (error.message.includes("P2025")) {
      // Record not found (for update/delete on missing record)
      return json({ error: "Quiz not found" }, { status: 404 });
    }
    if (error.message.includes("P2003")) {
      // Foreign key constraint (relation doesn't exist)
      return json({ error: "Related record not found" }, { status: 400 });
    }
  }
  return json({ error: "Database error" }, { status: 500 });
}
```

---

## SELF-VALIDATION CHECKLIST

- [ ] Every loader: try/catch → `throw json({ error }, { status })`
- [ ] Every action: try/catch → `return json({ error }, { status: 500 })`
- [ ] Every JSON.parse: wrapped in try/catch with fallback (Law 6)
- [ ] Public API routes: CORS headers on ALL error responses (Law 5)
- [ ] Prisma errors: P2002 (duplicate), P2025 (not found) handled explicitly
- [ ] External API calls: caught, logged, never bubble to user as 500
- [ ] Error boundary exported from route files
- [ ] `console.error()` used for logging (not `console.log()`)

---

## NEVER

- Never call `JSON.parse()` without try/catch (Law 6)
- Never let external API failures (Klaviyo, Shopify) crash the main flow
- Never swallow errors silently — always log to console.error
- Never return raw database errors to the client (hide internals)
- Never throw in action functions — return json({ error }) instead
- Never forget CORS headers on error responses in public API routes

